## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

